[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Logical Properties and Writing Modes for Internationalization in CSS3"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Internationalization in CSS"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Building modern web applications requires supporting multiple languages and writing systems, such as Left-to-Right (LTR) languages like English, Right-to-Left (RTL) languages like Arabic, and vertical scripts like traditional Japanese. Traditionally, CSS relied on physical properties like "left", "right", "top", and "bottom", which assume a horizontal LTR layout. CSS3 introduces logical properties and values that map to the flow of text rather than the physical screen edges."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Writing Modes"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"The writing-mode property defines whether the content flows horizontally or vertically and the direction in which lines progress. By changing this single property, the entire layout coordinate system adapts."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"horizontal-tb: Left-to-right or right-to-left horizontal text; lines flow top to bottom."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"vertical-rl: Vertical text; lines flow right to left."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"vertical-lr: Vertical text; lines flow left to right."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":".container {
  writing-mode: vertical-rl;
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Physical vs. Logical Properties"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Instead of defining margins, padding, and borders based on physical directions (top, right, bottom, left), logical properties use flow-relative terms: block and inline. The block dimension is the direction in which blocks are stacked, while the inline dimension is the direction in which text flows within a line."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"margin-left / margin-right become margin-inline-start / margin-inline-end"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"padding-top / padding-bottom become padding-block-start / padding-block-end"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"width / height become inline-size / block-size"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Practical Implementation Example"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following CSS snippet demonstrates how to style a card component using logical properties. This ensures the component automatically flips its spacing and alignments when the document's direction attribute changes from "ltr" to "rtl"."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":".card {
  writing-mode: horizontal-tb;
  inline-size: 300px;
  block-size: auto;
  padding-block: 1.5rem;
  padding-inline: 2rem;
  margin-inline-start: auto;
  margin-inline-end: auto;
  border-inline-start: 4px solid #007bff;
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Embracing CSS logical properties and writing modes is essential for scalable internationalized web design. It eliminates the need for complex overrides or separate CSS stylesheets for RTL languages, resulting in cleaner, more maintainable codebases."}]
